PLACES=[
N('wallace-historic-district-id','Wallace historic district','Wallace','id','town','standing',47.4723,-115.9262,'area','public','walk','','1890–',
  'Wallace grew where the routes from the Burke and Mullan mines met after the lead-silver strikes of 1884 (depot Register form), and much of its centre was built in 1890 and 1891 (Wikipedia). The whole town was put on the National Register in 1979, in a fight to stop Interstate 90 from being driven through the old centre; the listing was enlarged in 1983 to take in about 500 houses, and trimmed in 1990. The district counts 485 contributing buildings in its first listing and 301 more in the enlargement (Wikipedia).',
  'A downtown of Classical Revival, Renaissance Revival and Late Victorian commercial blocks, with streets of miners\' houses climbing the canyon sides (Wikipedia). The depot, the library and the post office have their own entries here. Most buildings are private homes or businesses: look from the street. Interiors not checked.',
  'The town that was listed whole to keep a freeway off its main street.',
  'A living town: walk the public streets, which are city streets. '+WALLACE,
  [W('Wallace Historic District'),WHD],brick='?',th=[47.47193,-115.92516],thn='Bank Street lot, downtown Wallace'),
N('northern-pacific-depot-wallace','Northern Pacific Railway Depot (railroad museum), Wallace','Wallace','id','rail','standing',47.47324,-115.92357,'exact','open','walk','stations','1901–',
  'The Northern Pacific\'s brick and concrete depot in Wallace, built to the company\'s plan 281-1; the Register form says it was built in 1901 and opened on December 29, 1901, while the Wikipedia list of Shoshone County\'s Register places gives 1906. On May 10, 1986 the state highway department moved it about 200 feet across the river to make room for the Interstate 90 viaduct (Register form; the foundation\'s page). It is now the Northern Pacific Depot Railroad Museum, run by the Northern Pacific Depot Foundation.',
  'A two-storey depot in the Chateau style: a cut-stone band at the foundation, a brick first storey with brick segmental arches over the windows, and a stuccoed concrete second storey with a tower (Register form). The form says its brick matches that of an unfinished Northern Pacific hotel begun in Tacoma in 1891. Inside is the railroad museum; the depot is also a trailhead for the Trail of the Coeur d\'Alenes.',
  '',
  'Open as a museum, with an admission fee; hours from the Northern Pacific Depot Foundation\'s page (npdepot.org), which says it opens off-season on request and for Wallace\'s winter events. Free parking at the depot (same page). 219 Sixth Street.',
  [['Northern Pacific Depot Railroad Museum','https://npdepot.org/about-the-museum/'],NR('76000681','Northern Pacific Railway Depot')],
  conf='mixed',brick='?',hours='April 15 to October 15: Monday to Saturday 9 a.m. to 5 p.m., Sunday 10 a.m. to 4 p.m.; off-season on request'),
N('wallace-carnegie-library-id','Wallace Carnegie Library','Wallace','id','civic','standing',47.47417,-115.92611,'exact','open','walk','','1910–1911',
  'Wallace was given $12,000 by the Carnegie foundation for a library in 1910, and the building was finished in April 1911 (Register form). It has been the town library ever since, and the Idaho Heritage Trust calls it one of four Carnegie libraries in Idaho still in use as libraries.',
  'A small neo-classical red brick building (the photograph on its Wikipedia article) on a high basement scored to look like rusticated stone, with quoined brick corners and window surrounds and an entrance portico with Ionic columns at the top of a flight of concrete steps (Register form; Wikipedia). 415 River Street, beside a small city park. Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'The working Wallace Public Library: open to the public in library hours, which were not found on a page of its own. '+WALLACE,
  [W('Wallace Carnegie Library'),NR('81000209','Wallace Carnegie Library'),['Idaho Heritage Trust: Wallace Carnegie Library','https://www.idahoheritagetrust.org/projects-grants/wallace-carnegie-library/']],
  brick='yes',hours='?'),
N('wallace-post-office-id','U.S. Post Office, Wallace','Wallace','id','civic','standing',47.472617,-115.926711,'site','view','walk','','1935–1936',
  'Wallace\'s first federally built post office, paid for under the emergency construction act of June 1934 and built in 1935 and 1936 (Register form, from the Wallace Press-Times). It was given an addition in the mid-1960s, and the form of 1988 calls the original design well preserved.',
  'A flattened Classical Revival front of buff brick with sandstone trim: a sandstone basement and water table, belt course, flat pilasters and window surrounds, limestone panels below the sills, and five concrete steps between sandstone buttresses up to a door with a wooden transom and keystone (Register form). Whether it is still a working post office, and its interior, not checked.',
  '',
  'Look from the street. '+WALLACE,
  [NR('89000137','U.S. Post Office, Wallace Main')],brick='no'),
N('pulaski-tunnel-wallace','Pulaski Tunnel (1910 fire refuge) and Placer Creek escape route','Wallace','id','industry','traces',47.44506,-115.96294,'area','public','hike','burned','1910',
  'On August 20, 1910, in the Great Fire, Forest Service ranger Ed Pulaski led his crew of about forty-five firefighters and two horses into a short abandoned prospecting tunnel beside the West Fork of Placer Creek as the fire surrounded them (Wikipedia). The Forest Service page says all but six of the 45 men were saved. A citizens\' group formed in 2002 to restore the tunnel and the trail, and the Forest Service built the trail from 2005 (Wikipedia).',
  'A two-mile trail (four miles there and back, about 800 feet of climb) with five footbridges and a dozen interpretive signs ends at an overlook across the creek from the Nicholson mine adit, the tunnel itself (Forest Service; Wikipedia). The opening was 6 feet high and 5 feet wide (Wikipedia). You look at the tunnel from the overlook.',
  '',
  'Idaho Panhandle National Forests. The trailhead and its parking area are on Forest Road 456 about a mile south of Wallace, on the left (Forest Service page); there is a restroom (Wikipedia). Foot traffic only. Allow two to four hours. Trail and trailhead conditions vary with the season (Forest Service).',
  [['Forest Service: Pulaski Tunnel Trail','https://www.fs.usda.gov/r01/idahopanhandle/recreation/trails/pulaski-tunnel-trail'],W('Edward Pulaski Tunnel and Placer Creek Escape Route')],
  brick='?',th=[47.45927,-115.93505],thn='Pulaski Tunnel trailhead, Forest Road 456'),
N('wallace-1910-fire-memorial','Wallace 1910 Fire Memorial, Nine Mile Cemetery','Wallace','id','burial','standing',47.487704,-115.915564,'site','public','walk','burned','1921',
  'Two cobblestone monuments put up in 1921 over the graves of firefighters killed in the Great Fire of 1910, called the lower and upper monuments from their places on the hill (Register form). The Nine Mile Cemetery Association owned the plots when they were listed in 1984.',
  'The lower monument is a D-shaped wall of large cobblestones set in concrete, about twelve feet across the front and four and a half feet high; the upper one is built the same way, and each carries a bronze plate with the names of the men buried there (Register form). Exact place in the cemetery not checked.',
  '',
  'Nine Mile Cemetery, north of Wallace up Nine Mile Creek; owned by the Nine Mile Cemetery Association (Register form, 1984). Cemetery hours and parking not checked: park along the cemetery roads and keep off the graves.',
  [NR('84001180','Wallace 1910 Fire Memorial')],brick='no'),
N('st-andrews-episcopal-mullan','St. Andrew\'s Episcopal Church (old Hunter school), Mullan','Mullan','id','worship','standing',47.470095,-115.802944,'site','view','walk','','1888',
  'Built on Hunter Avenue in the late summer of 1888 as Mullan\'s schoolhouse. When the school district built a bigger school, it let the building go to the Episcopal parish, which turned it into a church in 1892. It served as St. Andrew\'s until it was sold to a private owner in 1982, and when it was listed in 1999 a historical society of the same name owned it and work was in progress (Register form).',
  'A one-storey plank-framed building with a front gable, set on wooden posts on new concrete piers screened with vertical boards. The Gothic windows and door were put in at the conversion of 1892. The form describes a plain interior with simple trim, plain pews and a small altar and pulpit. 104 Hunter Avenue. Whether it opens now not checked.',
  '',
  'Look from Hunter Avenue. Ownership after 1999 and any opening days not checked. Street parking in Mullan, off Interstate 90 at exit 68.',
  [NR('99000419','St. Andrew\'s Episcopal Church, Mullan')],brick='no'),
N('murray-idaho','Murray gold camp','Murray','id','town','standing',47.62722,-115.85861,'area','public','drive','','1884–',
  'One of the boisterous mining camps of the 1880s in northern Idaho, on Prichard Creek about 20 miles north of Wallace over Dobson Pass; mines worked round it from the 1880s to the 1950s (Wikipedia). It was the Shoshone County seat until 1898, when the seat went to Wallace (courthouse Register form). Wikipedia gives it about 500 people in 1909 and about 25 in 2024.',
  'A thin street along Prichard Creek among dredge spoils. The 1885 courthouse, a two-storey clapboard building with a brick records vault, collapsed under snow in 1997 and was rebuilt as a replica (Register form; Wikipedia list). The Masonic hall has its own entry here; the John C. Feehan house, a one-storey hewn-log cabin of between 1884 and 1900, was listed in 1980 (Register form), and whether it still stands was not checked.',
  'The Earp connection: a judge fined Wyatt Earp $65 here for claim jumping in 1884 (Wikipedia).',
  'A small living community on the Dobson Pass road; look from the public road. Buildings are private unless they say otherwise.',
  [W('Murray, Idaho'),NR('78001098','Murray Courthouse'),NR('80001334','John C. Feehan House')],brick='?'),
N('murray-masonic-hall','Murray Masonic Hall','Murray','id','civic','standing',47.626832,-115.858062,'site','view','drive','','1884–1886',
  'Built between 1884 and 1886, soon after Murray began, for the Masonic lodge (Register form, 1987). Its side walls were reclad and its stair moved outside about 1957.',
  'A two-and-a-half-storey frame hall, 24 by 36 feet, on the south side of Main Street, with a symmetrical upper front of four two-over-two windows under moulded wooden lintels with keystones; the boards and battens survive on the west wall and rear gable, and the storefront below has a modern door under its old two-paned transom. Upstairs the lodge room kept its original kerosene wall lamps on elaborate metal hangers (Register form, 1987). Interior not seen by any later source; not open as far as any source says.',
  '',
  'Look from Main Street. Owner and any opening not checked.',
  [NR('87000774','Murray Masonic Hall')],brick='no'),
N('pine-creek-baptist-church-pinehurst','Pine Creek Baptist Church (log church), Pinehurst','Pinehurst','id','worship','standing',47.53871,-116.23684,'site','view','drive','','1932',
  'A log church designed in 1932 by the Boise firm of Tourtellotte and Hummel, their most northerly known work in Idaho and the only true log building among the works listed with them in 1982 (Register form). It was then called the Pinehurst Baptist Church.',
  'Solid log walls, front gables covered with rustic shingles and a small square belfry with a pyramid roof; front and rear additions in matching logs. The form says the interior still showed its exposed log walls and baptistry, under newer ceilings, carpet and pews. Whether services are held now, and the interior today, not checked.',
  '',
  'Look from the street; Main Street at the corner with South Fourth (Register form, 1982). Off Interstate 90 at Pinehurst.',
  [NR('82000361','Pine Creek Baptist Church')],brick='no',hours='?'),
N('avery-depot-id','Avery Depot (Milwaukee Road)','Avery','id','rail','standing',47.250797,-115.807348,'exact','open','drive','stations','1910',
  'The Milwaukee Road\'s depot at Avery on the St. Joe River, dated 1910 by its Register form. When the railroad went bankrupt in the 1980s the depot was sold to the town for a community centre, and Wikipedia says it now serves as community centre, museum, post office and library.',
  'A long one-storey frame depot with the passenger and office end at one side and the freight end at the other, two-tone horizontal and vertical siding, wide eaves on large figure-four brackets along the track side, one-over-one windows and three interior brick chimneys (Register form, 1984). Interior not checked.',
  '',
  'Owned by the Avery Citizens Committee when listed (Register form, 1984). Avery is on the St. Joe River Road (Forest Highway 50). Opening times of the museum, post office and library were not found.',
  [W('Avery station'),NR('84001142','Avery Depot')],brick='?',hours='?'),
N('route-of-the-hiawatha-id','Route of the Hiawatha (Milwaukee Road over St. Paul Pass)','Avery','id','rail','traces',47.36097,-115.64959,'area','open','walk','','1906–1980',
  'The Milwaukee Road, the last transcontinental railroad, began its Pacific Extension in 1906 and crossed the Bitterroots by a tunnel under St. Paul Pass, 8,771 feet long, finished in 1908; the line over the pass needed 14 tunnels and 26 bridges, and was electrified from 1914 (Wikipedia). The railroad abandoned it in 1980. The right of way from East Portal, Montana to Loop Creek near Pearson is a National Register district, and the Route of the Hiawatha trail now runs along it; the tunnel was rededicated as part of the trail on June 23, 2001 (Wikipedia).',
  'Fifteen miles of gentle downhill grade through 10 tunnels, the first the 1.66-mile St. Paul Pass (Taft) tunnel, and over 7 high steel trestles (the trail\'s page). Wikipedia says the bridges and tunnels still stand. The tunnels are cold, dark and muddy: take lights and layers (the trail\'s page).',
  '',
  'A ticketed trail run from Lookout Pass Ski Area, with shuttles back up and bike rentals by reservation; the main trailhead is East Portal, also called the Taft trailhead, off Interstate 90 at exit 5 (Taft) in Montana, and the ride ends at Pearson in Idaho (ridethehiawatha.com). The page said "closed for the season" when read in November 2026 and gave no season dates. Pin is the Register point for the district in Idaho; the parking point is a lot on the trail mapped in OpenStreetMap, inside St. Joe National Forest.',
  [['Route of the Hiawatha','https://www.ridethehiawatha.com/'],W('Chicago, Milwaukee, St. Paul and Pacific Railroad Company Historic District'),W('St. Paul Pass Tunnel')],
  brick='?',hours='?',th=[47.36157,-115.65369],thn='Trail parking (OpenStreetMap), St. Joe National Forest'),
N('cataldo-mission-id','Mission of the Sacred Heart (Cataldo Mission)','Cataldo','id','worship','standing',47.5487,-116.3582,'site','open','walk','','1850–1853',
  'The Jesuit mission to the Coeur d\'Alene people moved here in 1846 from a flood-prone site on the St. Joe River. Father Antonio Ravalli designed the church, and the Coeur d\'Alene built it from 1850 to 1853 by wattle and daub, without nails; it is the oldest standing building in Idaho (Wikipedia). It was made a National Historic Landmark in 1961 and restored for the Bicentennial in 1976, and is now Coeur d\'Alene\'s Old Mission State Park.',
  'Inside, Wikipedia describes walls once hung with Hudson\'s Bay Company cloth and hand-painted newspaper, tin-can chandeliers, two wooden statues carved by a priest, and woodwork stained blue with pressed huckleberries. The park also holds the restored parish house, a historic cemetery and the Sacred Encounters exhibit; rangers lead tours of the church (state park page).',
  'Idaho\'s oldest building, put up without a nail.',
  'Idaho Department of Parks and Recreation. Motor vehicle entrance fee $7, exhibit fee $5 a person or $10 a family; closed major holidays in winter (state park page). Off Interstate 90 at Cataldo, about 28 miles east of Coeur d\'Alene.',
  [['Idaho Parks and Recreation: Old Mission','https://parksandrecreation.idaho.gov/parks/coeur-d-alenes-old-mission/'],W('Old Mission State Park')],
  brick='no',hours='April to October 9 a.m. to 5 p.m.; November to March 10 a.m. to 3 p.m.'),
N('burke-idaho','Burke ghost town (Burke Canyon)','Burke','id','town','ruins',47.52028,-115.82028,'area','view','drive','','1887–1991',
  'A silver, lead and zinc camp founded in 1887 in a canyon only about 300 feet wide, home of the Hercules, Hecla and Star mines and a centre of the 1892 strike and the mining wars of 1899 (Wikipedia). The 150-room Tiger Hotel of 1887 straddled the creek with the railroad running through its lobby (HistoryNet); it was torn down in 1944 (Spokane Historical). Mining ended in 1991 with the closing of the Star mine, and by 2002 nobody lived in Burke itself (Wikipedia).',
  'Decaying mine buildings of the Hecla Mining Company line the canyon (Spokane Historical); pictures on the Wikipedia article show a Hecla building, a skywalk between buildings and the Star mine headframe. Wikipedia says Hecla was exploring the Star mine again around 2010 to 2012. What stands today not checked on the ground.',
  'A town so narrow the train went through the hotel lobby.',
  'About 7 miles northeast of Wallace on the Burke-Canyon Creek Road (State Highway 4) (Wikipedia). The mine buildings belong to a mining company: look from the road and keep off the property. Ownership of each building not checked.',
  [W('Burke, Idaho'),['Spokane Historical: The Ghost Town of Burke','https://spokanehistorical.org/items/show/517'],['HistoryNet: Ghost Towns: Burke, Idaho','https://www.historynet.com/ghost-towns-burke-idaho.htm']],
  conf='mixed',brick='?'),
N('sierra-silver-mine-tour-wallace','Sierra Silver Mine Tour','Wallace','id','industry','standing',47.47265,-115.92493,'site','open','walk','','',
  'A guided underground tour run by Sierra Silver Mine Tour, Inc., which says its aim is to teach the history and practice of hard-rock mining in the Coeur d\'Alene district. Visitors ride a trolley from the ticket office in Wallace to the mine and back, with a narrated tour of the town on the way (its page). The mine\'s own history was not read in a source.',
  'A 75-minute tour, part underground; tours leave every half hour (its page). What machinery is shown not checked.',
  '',
  'Tickets at the tour office, 509 Cedar Street, Wallace; adult, senior ($22), child ($12.50) and family ($75) prices on its page. The pages read in November 2026 disagree on the season end (October 15 on one, October on the other) and on October hours. Pin is the ticket office; the mine itself, reached by the tour trolley, was not located. '+WALLACE,
  [['Sierra Silver Mine Tour','https://silverminetour.org/take-the-mine-tour/']],
  conf='mixed',brick='?',hours='Daily May 1 to October 15; tours 10 a.m. to 4 p.m. June to August, 10 a.m. to 2 p.m. May and September'),
]

